utation in PAPSS2. They found loss-of-function PAPSS2 mutations in 3 more patients with similar phenotypes (2 Japanese and 1 Korean).","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["We examined three affected individuals of the family using exon capture followed by next generation sequencing and identified its disease gene, PAPSS2 (phosphoadenosine-phosphosulfate synthetase 2)."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:22791835:2","pmid":"22791835","section":"genotype_phenotype_correlations","statement":"Miyake et al. (2012) noted that spinal changes were very similar among the 6 patients, but epiphyseal and metaphyseal changes of the tubular bones varied. They concluded that PAPSS2 mutations cause a family of skeletal dysplasias, with phenotypes ranging from brachyolmia to spondyloepimetaphyseal dysplasia.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["Spinal changes were very similar among the six patients; however, epiphyseal and metaphyseal changes of the tubular bones were variable.","PAPSS2 mutations have produced a skeletal dysplasia family, with a gradation of phenotypes ranging from brachyolmia to spondylo-epi-metaphyseal dysplasia."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:22791835:3","pmid":"22791835","section":"clinical_features","statement":"Miyake et al. (2012) reported that the 6 patients had short-trunk short stature that became noticeable in childhood, with normal intelligence and facies. Radiographs showed rectangular vertebral bodies with irregular endplates, narrow intervertebral discs, early calcification of rib cartilage, short femoral necks, and mildly short metacarpals.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["The six patients had short-trunk short stature that became conspicuous during childhood with normal intelligence and facies.","Their radiographic features included rectangular vertebral bodies with irregular endplates and narrow intervertebral discs, precocious calcification of rib cartilages, short femoral neck, and mildly shortened metacarpals."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:19474428:1","pmid":"19474428","section":"molecular_genetics","statement":"Noordam et al. (2009) showed that the PAPSS2 mutations were inactivating: when human SULT2A1 was coincubated in vitro with wild-type or mutant PAPSS2 proteins, the mutant proteins failed to support SULT2A1 activity. They concluded that PAPSS2 deficiency is a monogenic adrenocortical cause of androgen excess.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["In vitro coincubation of human SULT2A1 and wild-type or mutant PAPSS2 proteins confirmed the inactivating nature of the mutations.","These observations indicate that PAPSS2 deficiency is a monogenic adrenocortical cause of androgen excess."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:19474428:2","pmid":"19474428","section":"pathogenesis","statement":"Noordam et al. (2009) noted that DHEA sulfotransferase (SULT2A1) converts DHEA to its inactive sulfate, DHEAS, and needs PAPS, which PAPSS2 makes, to work. As a result, PAPSS2 deficiency lowers DHEA sulfation and allows more DHEA to be converted to active androgens.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["Dehydroepiandrosterone (DHEA) sulfotransferase, known as SULT2A1, converts the androgen precursor DHEA to its inactive sulfate ester, DHEAS [corrected], thereby preventing the conversion of DHEA to an active androgen.","SULT2A1 requires 3'-phosphoadenosine-5'-phosphosulfate (PAPS) for catalytic activity."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:9771708:1","pmid":"9771708","section":"mapping","statement":"Faiyaz ul Haque et al. (1998) mapped the gene for a recessively inherited SEMD in a large inbred Pakistani family to chromosome 10q23-24, a region syntenic with the brachymorphic locus on mouse chromosome 19.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["mapped a gene associated with this dwarfing condition to chromosome 10q23-24, a region syntenic with the locus for the brachymorphic mutation on mouse chromosome 19"],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:9771708:2","pmid":"9771708","section":"animal_model","statement":"Faiyaz ul Haque et al. (1998) identified a missense mutation in the region of Atpsk2 encoding the APS kinase activity in the brachymorphic mouse, the orthologue of human ATPSK2.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["a missense mutation in the region of Atpsk2 encoding the APS kinase activity in the brachymorphic mouse"],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:9771708:3","pmid":"9771708","section":"pathogenesis","statement":"Faiyaz ul Haque et al. (1998) noted that ATP sulfurylase/APS kinase activates inorganic sulfate to PAPS, the universal sulfate donor, and that the cartilage specificity of the human and mouse phenotypes supported a critical role of sulfate activation in maturation of cartilage extracellular matrix and skeletogenesis.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["ATP sulfurylase/APS kinase catalyses the metabolic activation of inorganic sulfate to PAPS, the universal donor for post-translational protein sulfation in all cell types.","The cartilage-specificity of the human and mouse phenotypes provides further evidence of the critical role of sulfate activation in the maturation of cartilage extracellular matrix molecules"],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:9714015:1","pmid":"9714015","section":"inheritance","statement":"Ahmad et al. (1998) concluded that pedigree analysis of an 8-generation inbred Pakistani kindred strongly suggested autosomal recessive inheritance, with consanguineous loops able to account for all affected individuals being homozygous for the abnormal allele.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["Analysis of the pedigree strongly suggests autosomal recessive inheritance, and consanguineous loops could account for all the affected individuals being homozygous for the abnormal allele."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:9714015:2","pmid":"9714015","section":"clinical_features","statement":"Ahmad et al. (1998) considered this SEMD phenotype distinct from other autosomal recessive forms of SEMD because of the mild degree of metaphyseal involvement, the type of brachydactyly, and the absence of loose joints or other clinical findings.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["This distinctive phenotype is distinct from other autosomal recessive forms of SEMD because of the mild degree of metaphyseal involvement, the type of brachydactyly, and the absence of loose joints or other clinical findings."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]}],"dropped":{"records":[],"notes":[]},"gene_decisions":{"candidates":["PAPSS2"],"confirmed":["PAPSS2"],"dropped":[],"not_confirmed":[]},"candidate_inheritance":["AR"]}